{"id": "q0314", "pool": "easy-fact", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 5.1, "n_chunks": 6, "scores": [0.5512, 0.5199, 0.4703, 0.4921, 0.4433, 0.4573], "eot_score": 0.6719, "eot_read_ms": 24, "reference_answer": "Cole Porter", "query": "\"Who wrote the sons \"\"I've Got You Under My Skin\"\" and \"\"I Get A Kick Out Of You\"\"?\"", "transcript": "Who wrote the songs I've Got You Under My Skin and I Get a Kick Out of You?", "mode": "local", "answer": "The song \"I've Got You Under My Skin\" was written by **Cole Porter**. It is one of his most famous compositions and has been recorded by many artists over the years, including Frank Sinatra, whose version became particularly iconic.", "answer_ms": 1187}
{"id": "q0321", "pool": "easy-fact", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 3.38, "n_chunks": 4, "scores": [0.3848, 0.4549, 0.5268, 0.4526], "eot_score": 0.7795, "eot_read_ms": 22, "reference_answer": "EMMA BUNTON (Baby Spice)", "query": "Which of the 'Spice Girls' advertised 'Milky Way' ob t.v.?", "transcript": "Which of the Spice Girls advertised Milky Way on TV?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Emma Bunton — \"Baby Spice\" — advertised Milky Way on TV.", "expert_answer": "Emma Bunton — “Baby Spice” — advertised Milky Way on TV.", "expert_latency_s": 12.25, "stall_ms": 30, "relay_ms": 423}
{"id": "q0344", "pool": "easy-fact", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 6.66, "n_chunks": 7, "scores": [0.3815, 0.504, 0.4663, 0.4606, 0.302, 0.3604, 0.4064], "eot_score": 0.5282, "eot_read_ms": 24, "reference_answer": "CRETE", "query": "\"Which country is the setting for the 1957 film, \"\"Ill Met By Moonlight\"\", starring Dirk Bogarde?\"", "transcript": "Which country is the setting for the 1957 film Ill Met By Moonlight, starring Dirk Bogarde?", "mode": "local", "answer": "The 1957 film *Ill Met by Moonlight*, starring Dirk Bogarde, is set in **Italy**. The story takes place during the late 18th century and revolves around themes of love, betrayal, and political intrigue against the backdrop of Italian society at the time.", "answer_ms": 1496}
{"id": "q0353", "pool": "easy-fact", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 3.9, "n_chunks": 4, "scores": [0.4219, 0.3902, 0.3039, 0.389], "eot_score": 0.6429, "eot_read_ms": 23, "reference_answer": "The Fates", "query": "Who were the Greek equivalents of the Norns of Norse mythology?", "transcript": "Who are the Greek equivalents of the Norns of Norse mythology?", "mode": "local", "answer": "In Greek mythology, the closest equivalents to the **Norns** of Norse mythology—those mysterious female figures who shape fate and destiny in the Norse tradition—are primarily found among the **Moirai** or **Moerai**, also known as the **Fates** or **Parcae** in Roman tradition. These deities are central to concepts of fate and destiny across Indo-European mythologies, including both Greek and Norse traditions, though their roles and names differ slightly between cultures. Here's a breakdown of the key similarities and distinctions between the Norns and their Greek counterparts, particularly the Moirai and other related figures like the **Clotho, Lachesis, and Atropos** from later traditions associated with the Fates, as well as other female figures involved in weaving fate and destiny in Greek mythology such as the **Clotho**, **Lachesis**, and sometimes even **Rhea** or **Athena** in certain contexts involving prophecy and cosmic order.", "answer_ms": 5226}
{"id": "q0361", "pool": "easy-fact", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 7.86, "n_chunks": 8, "scores": [0.4595, 0.3829, 0.3949, 0.451, 0.4654, 0.3973, 0.6275, 0.4173], "eot_score": 0.7381, "eot_read_ms": 21, "reference_answer": "False teeth", "query": "In Vermont, USA, there is an old law stating that it is illegal for women to wear what without their husbands written consent?", "transcript": "In Vermont, USA, there is an old law stating that it is illegal for women to wear what without their husband's written consent?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "In Vermont, an old law supposedly stated that women could not wear **false teeth** without their husband’s written consent.", "expert_answer": "In Vermont, an old law supposedly stated that women could not wear **false teeth** without their husband’s written consent.", "expert_latency_s": 2.32, "stall_ms": 28, "relay_ms": 632}
{"id": "q0366", "pool": "easy-fact", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 4.6, "n_chunks": 5, "scores": [0.4732, 0.4803, 0.4719, 0.4056, 0.4554], "eot_score": 0.6909, "eot_read_ms": 22, "reference_answer": "His arm", "query": "Apart from a battle, what did Nelson lose at Tenerife in 1797?", "transcript": "Apart from a battle, what did Nelson lose at Teneriffe in 1797?", "mode": "local", "answer": "In 1797, during the failed invasion of Tenerife as part of the Spanish Treasure Fleet campaign, Admiral Horatio Nelson lost one of his most trusted and capable officers—Captain William Parker—to illness. Parker had been suffering from fever-like symptoms for some time but continued to serve with distinction despite being weakened by disease. He eventually succumbed to illness aboard the ship *Macedonian*, highlighting not only the physical toll on the crew in tropical conditions but also the personal cost of leadership during this ill-fated expedition.", "answer_ms": 2663}
{"id": "q0383", "pool": "easy-fact", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 6.09, "n_chunks": 7, "scores": [0.4489, 0.4365, 0.4654, 0.4406, 0.3271, 0.3887, 0.465], "eot_score": 0.4955, "eot_read_ms": 21, "reference_answer": "Benjamin Franklin", "query": "Who flew a kite in a storm on June 15, 1750, proving that lightning was electrical?", "transcript": "Who flew a kite in a storm on June fifteen, seventeen fifty, proving that lightning was electrical?", "mode": "local", "answer": "Benjamin Franklin flew a kite in a storm on June 15, 1750, to demonstrate that lightning was electrical. While the exact date of this specific experiment may vary slightly depending on sources, it is widely associated with his famous kite experiment during a thunderstorm, which helped prove the connection between lightning and electricity.", "answer_ms": 1543}
{"id": "q0390", "pool": "easy-fact", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 16.09, "n_chunks": 17, "scores": [0.4194, 0.4243, 0.5336, 0.3935, 0.4223, 0.2656, 0.2175, 0.3854, 0.3193, 0.3062, 0.4303, 0.5186, 0.5066, 0.3344, 0.5404, 0.4349, 0.5322], "eot_score": 0.68, "eot_read_ms": 22, "reference_answer": "Nokia tune", "query": "\"The 1902 guitar composition \"\"Gran Vals\"\" by the Spanish composer Francisco Tárrega would have remained obscure but for corporate executive Anssi Vanjoki selecting it for something in 1993. How do millions of people recognize it now?\"", "transcript": "The 1902 guitar composition \"Gran Vals\" by the Spanish composer Francisco Tarrega would have remained obscure, but for corporate executive Anse Van Yockey selecting it for \"Something\" in 1993. How do millions of people recognize it now?", "mode": "local", "answer": "Millions of people recognize *Gran Vals*, the 1902 guitar composition by Francisco Tárrega, primarily due to its use in popular culture—most notably as the theme music for the iconic TV series *The Simpsons*. In 1993, Anse Van Wyck, a corporate executive and co-founder of the company that produced *The Simpsons*, selected the piece because he admired its emotional depth and beauty. The hauntingly beautiful melody became closely associated with Homer Simpson’s character, especially during his moments of longing or nostalgia, such as when he sings while looking at photos from his youth or reflecting on simpler times. This exposure through one of the most-watched animated shows in history brought widespread attention to the piece, making it instantly recognizable across generations and cultures. Today, even those who don’t know much about classical guitar often hum or recognize fragments of *Gran Vals*, thanks largely to this cultural connection.", "answer_ms": 4921}
{"id": "q0397", "pool": "easy-fact", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 2.04, "n_chunks": 3, "scores": [0.4702, 0.3331, 0.3616], "eot_score": 0.7214, "eot_read_ms": 22, "reference_answer": "A business", "query": "What is a group of ferrets called?", "transcript": "What is a group of ferrets called?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "A group of ferrets is called a business.", "expert_answer": "A group of ferrets is called a **business**.", "expert_latency_s": 0.98, "stall_ms": 29, "relay_ms": 267}
{"id": "q0410", "pool": "easy-chat", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 0.91, "n_chunks": 1, "scores": [0.3415], "eot_score": 0.6543, "eot_read_ms": 27, "reference_answer": null, "query": "What is fleece?", "transcript": "What is fleece?", "mode": "local", "answer": "\"Fleece\" can refer to several different things depending on the context. Here are the most common meanings of the word \"fleece\" or the phrase \"to fleece\" someone or something in everyday usage and technical contexts, particularly related to animals and textiles or scams and deception. I'll cover both senses clearly below. If you meant a specific context, please let me know for a more precise answer.### 1. Fleece as Animal Wool or Fur### 1.1. Wool from Sheep or Other Animals#### Meaning:- **Fleece** refers to the soft wool or hair covering an animal's body, especially sheep.#### Examples:- \"Sheep are shorn annually to harvest their fleece.\"- \"The new coat is made from pure wool fleece.\"#### Key Points:- **Sheep fleece**: The thick layer of wool that grows on sheep; it's typically harvested by shearing.- **Other animals**: Some animals like alpacas, llamas, goats, and rabbits also produce fleece-like fibers used in textiles.- **Quality**: Fleece can vary in quality based on breed, climate, and how well it was cared for.### 2. To Fleece Someone – Meaning \"To Deceive or Scam\"### 2.1. To Defraud or Exploit#### Meaning:- **To fleece** someone means to deceive them into giving up money or property, often through fraud or exploitation.#### Examples:- \"He was fleeced out of his savings by a fake investment scheme.\"- \"The scammer faked an urgent call and successfully fleeced the victim.\"#### Key Points:- This usage implies **deception** rather than physical wool.- Often used with phrases like \"to fleece someone dry\" or \"fleeced out of money.\"- Common in contexts of **fraud, scams, or financial exploitation**.### Summary of \"Fleece\"### | Context | Meaning | Example | Notes |### |---|---|---|---|### | Textiles / Animals | Wool from sheep or other animals | \"The sweater is made from sheep fleece.\" | Natural fiber used in clothing and blankets. |### | Deception / Scams | To defraud or exploit someone financially | \"She was fleeced by a fake online seller.\" | Implies trickery or fraud. |If you were asking about a specific context—like fleece fabric for jackets, a type of scam, or animal husbandry—let me know and I can go deeper!", "answer_ms": 16010}
